From 9e456119d929dc8e05dca31f77de16e93d95b1e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Still <mikal@stillhq.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 17:26:16 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] Fix a connect crash with streams; hardware encoding on any GPU

This series fixes a crash that every spice-server tested hits when a
client sends its video codec preference while the guest is already
streaming, and then lets the GStreamer encoder use VA-API hardware
encoders on GPUs other than Intel's, falling back to software when a
hardware encoder cannot be used. It is based on master at adeb620a04.

Patch 1, the crash. A display client is on the channel's client list
from dcc_new(), but is given the existing streams only at the end of
dcc_start(). A SPICE_MSGC_DISPLAY_PREFERRED_VIDEO_CODEC_TYPE handled in
that window stops every stream, which queues a STREAM_CLIP for a stream
the new client never had, and the clip's destructor dereferences the
agent's NULL dcc. With streaming-video=all and a guest streaming before
the client connects, 0.15.2, 0.16.0 and master crashed in 20 of 20
connects; with the patch, 0 of 20. spice-gtk only sends the preference
when the application asks it to, which remote-viewer never does, so
remote-viewer does not hit this; a client that sends its preference at
connect hits it every time. There is no new test: nothing in
server/tests can link a display channel as a client.

Patches 2 and 3, hardware encoding. Patch 2 probes a hardware element
by taking it to READY before using it, and rebuilds a pipeline in
software when a hardware one cannot be built, started or encode, or
when the frame size is one the hardware encoder does not take. Patch 3
replaces the Intel-only udev check with a check for a DRM render node,
so GStreamer's va plugin is tried on AMD and other VA-API GPUs. They
are split for review: patch 2 alone changes nothing on hosts without
an Intel GPU.

How this was measured. A Linux guest on virtio-vga under KVM draws an
animated 640x480 region and, on each key press, a small box. The
client connects through a proxy over a veth link shaped with netem
(delay and rate, both directions). Latency is from a key press to the
client drawing the box, 40 presses per case, three repeats, with the
builds interleaved inside every pass so that they share the host's
drift. The host is an AMD Ryzen 7 8845HS (Radeon 780M, Phoenix) on
Debian 13, with Mesa 25.0.7's radeonsi VA-API driver and GStreamer
1.26. Each build is made with Debian's flags and swapped in under the
host's qemu with LD_LIBRARY_PATH; meson test passes after each patch.
Stock qemu's non-GL display path cuts damage into 32-pixel columns,
narrower than radeonsi's vah264enc accepts, so the encoder rows use a
qemu that sends each damaged region whole, in one 640x480 stream. The
client prefers H.264. Master plus patch 1, encoding with x264enc,
against the whole series, encoding with vah264enc:

                                     x264enc       vah264enc
  qemu CPU, cores                    0.15 - 0.16   0.13
  qemu peak RSS, MiB                 219 - 240     278 - 297
  80 ms / 10 Mbit, heavy, p50 / p95  186 / 1262    107 / 275 ms
  80 ms / 10 Mbit, heavy, display    7.3           5.5 Mbit/s

vah264enc encoded every H.264 stream, with no fallbacks. Latency on the
other links (20 ms / 50 Mbit, and 80 ms / 10 Mbit with less noise) did
not change. The extra memory is the VA driver loaded into qemu, and is
paid only once a GStreamer encoder is built: with spice:mjpeg streams
the series made no difference to RSS. With /dev/dri hidden from qemu,
or holding a render node that cannot be opened, streams were encoded
by x264enc, as were streams 32 pixels wide.

Two other changes were measured and dropped, since neither made a
difference here:

- Raising the stream trace ring from 8 to 64 entries. Under stock qemu,
  whose column slivers outnumber the ring, every build created the same
  streams, and 14 of 15 cells showed nothing. The ring is only searched
  when many drawables leave the tree at once.
- Raising MAX_FPS from 30 to 60. Streams were created at 60 fps as
  intended, but latency, CPU, bandwidth and messages per second did not
  change in any of 12 cells, with H.264 or MJPEG. The client received 4
  to 12 stream frames a second whatever the ceiling, so on these links
  rate control, not the ceiling, sets the frame rate.

